From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: selftests/mm: check that fsync() reports hard-poisoned dirty page-cache
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:38:21 -0800

MADV_HWPOISON on a dirty page-cache page records -EIO in the file's
mapping.  Filesystems whose ->flush() checks the mapping report that error
from close(): NFS, FUSE, CIFS and orangefs do, and overlayfs and ecryptfs
pass on whatever the lower filesystem's ->flush() returns.  The hard
dirty_pagecache variant ends with ASSERT_EQ(close(fd), 0), so on those
filesystems it fails.  From a FUSE directory:

  memory-failure.c:389:dirty_pagecache:Expected close(fd) (-1) == 0 (0)

Commit e14e52a7ce02 ("selftests/mm: skip hard dirty page-cache test on
NFS") handled this by skipping the variant on NFS, and the same has been
proposed for FUSE.  But the -EIO is the expected result of the injection,
and the test never checks that the kernel recorded it.  On filesystems
with no ->flush(), such as ext4, close() returns 0 and the error goes
unobserved.

So test for it.  After the hard variant, expect fsync() to fail with EIO.
That also consumes the error, so the close() which follows returns 0 on
filesystems which report the mapping error, and the NFS skip can go away.

NILFS2's nilfs_sync_file() does not check the mapping error: fsync()
returned 0 after hard poisoning.  Skip only this one NILFS2 case for
now.  The other NILFS2 page-cache tests still run, and the skip can be
removed when NILFS2 reports the error.

FUSE has no ->error_remove_folio(), so memory_failure() cannot remove the
poisoned dirty page.  It stays in the page cache until the inode is
evicted, which FUSE can do after close() has returned, and until then the
test's teardown cannot unpoison the page: unpoison_memory() failed with
-EBUSY in 6 of 40 runs.  Truncate the file after the fsync() check so the
page is gone before teardown.

The page-cache tests can only work on a filesystem which keeps its data on
backing store.  tmpfs was already skipped for that reason, but ramfs and
hugetlbfs are in the same position and the tests fail on both today.  Skip
all three, and say why in a comment.

memory-failure results on x86_64 under QEMU, with the working directory on
each filesystem.  FUSE was a minimal test server, not virtiofs.

                before            after
  ext4          10 pass           10 pass
  FUSE          9 pass, 1 fail    10 pass
  NFSv4.2       9 pass, 1 skip    10 pass
  NILFS2        9 pass, 1 fail    9 pass, 1 skip
  tmpfs         6 pass, 4 skip    6 pass, 4 skip
  ramfs         7 pass, 3 fail    6 pass, 4 skip
  hugetlbfs     6 pass, 4 fail    6 pass, 4 skip

Fixes: d51b5076c746 ("selftests/mm: add memory failure dirty pagecache test")
Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Reported-by: Kurt Lin <lylin1023@gmail.com>
Closes: https://lore.kernel.org/20261123-memory-failure-fuse-skip-v1-1-19dcbda6e2e8@gmail.com
